# TEMPORARY (2026-09-28, until the backend's HANDOFF.md queue is done): the LIGHT gate,
# `complete:light`. It keeps every static check (lint, format, both spec lints, generated-types
# freshness, spec identity, docs references, `vue-tsc`) plus the unit suite.
#
# Left out, so run the full `npm run complete` by hand before merging a lane and once on main after
# each merge (CI still runs all of it):
#
# - the Vite build and Cypress e2e against the demo dataset — most of the full gate's minutes;
# - unit coverage and its floors.
#
# Restoring the full gate: revert the commit that introduced this block.
#
# `test:e2e:live` is NOT here, and it is the gate that actually proves behaviour — it needs the
# paired backend, a Mongo and a Redis, so it runs in CI as a required job instead. A green commit
# hook is not a claim that this frontend agrees with that backend; the PR is.
#
# `check:spec-identity` SKIPS rather than fails when the paired backend is not on disk, so a
# half-cloned pair can still commit. It stays fatal under CI, where a missing sibling means the
# workflow is misconfigured rather than absent.
#
# Deliberately not here, and run by hand for the occasions that want them: `test:mutation`
# (re-executes the unit suite once per mutant — nightly in CI), and `complete:manual`, which is
# `test:e2e:visual` (pixel diffs that answer to the machine that took them) plus `test:e2e:live`
# (needs the paired backend up).
#
# FA124 — lint and format check the STAGED SNAPSHOT, not the working tree. `lint-staged`
# (`.lintstagedrc.json`) runs `eslint` and `prettier --check` on staged files only. Before this,
# both ran over the whole tree here: a peer's unrelated dirty file — or a scratch note sitting in
# the same checkout — could fail a commit that never touched it, and the `prettier --write` this
# hook used to run as part of `regenerate` rewrote every unformatted file in the tree, not only the
# ones being committed. `type-check-only` and `test:unit` below stay whole-project on purpose:
# `vue-tsc` needs the whole project graph regardless of what changed, and neither one can be tricked
# into failing by an unrelated dirty file the way a tree-wide format check can.
npx lint-staged

# `contracts/` is generated FROM `openapi.yaml` and `asyncapi.yaml`, so regenerating it is only
# ever needed when one of those two is staged — skipping otherwise is FA124's other half: this
# used to run `regenerate` and `git add contracts` on EVERY commit regardless of relevance,
# sweeping whatever a peer's unrelated, unstaged edit under `contracts/` happened to be sitting
# there into this commit.
if git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR | grep -qE '^(openapi|asyncapi)\.yaml$'; then
    npm run regenerate

    # A regenerated `contracts/` that still differs from the working tree means the staged
    # contract and the generated client disagree. Fail and say so, rather than silently
    # `git add`-ing whatever `regenerate` produced — the previous behaviour FA124 flagged as
    # "surprising and risky for anyone with unrelated uncommitted work sitting in the same
    # checkout."
    if ! git diff --quiet -- contracts; then
        echo ""
        echo "[pre-commit] contracts/ changed after \`npm run regenerate\` but isn't staged."
        echo "  Review the diff below, then \`git add contracts\` and commit again."
        echo ""
        git status --short -- contracts
        exit 1
    fi
fi

npm run complete:light:gates
